WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.900
When a visitor buzzes in, every resident's phone gets an alert.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:09.930
If anyone taps it within five minutes, the building's security audit counts it as acknowledged.

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:15.820
Across nine buildings, the audit's dashboard shows ninety-seven point three per cent acknowledged.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.760
A paper from the School of Continuous Improvement asks what that word actually captures.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.450
We interviewed sixty-four residents across those buildings.

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.860
We asked what they actually do the moment the alert appears.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:32.720
We sorted what every resident told us into one of four practices.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:41.670
Verify, then act; verify, then dismiss; a reflexive swipe; or muted entirely.

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:46.690
Among residents in their first year, three of eighteen swiped it away unread.

00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.334
Among those of three years or more, twelve of twenty-two:

00:00:50.534 --> 00:00:51.254
more than half.

00:00:52.054 --> 00:00:54.324
Then we read each building's own dashboard.

00:00:54.624 --> 00:00:58.104
The longer residents had lived there, the faster they acknowledged the alert.

00:00:58.904 --> 00:01:02.934
The median time fell from fifteen and a half seconds in the first year,

00:01:03.134 --> 00:01:04.898
to five and a half at three years or more.

00:01:05.698 --> 00:01:08.778
On the dashboard alone, that reads as improving compliance.

00:01:09.078 --> 00:01:12.458
Beside the interviews, it is residents doing less, faster.

00:01:13.358 --> 00:01:17.528
A five-minute window was built to ask whether someone checked the door.

00:01:17.828 --> 00:01:20.938
It was never built to notice that the fastest possible tap

00:01:21.088 --> 00:01:24.238
and the least attentive one have become the same gesture.
